s [Data: Entities (1952)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Hierarchical Grounding Distribution via TBB\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The Grounding Backbone Conductor (TBB) is instrumental in interconnecting the TMGB to multiple Telecommunications Grounding Busbars (TGBs), effectively creating a grounding backbone across different areas or levels of a facility [Data: Entities (1953), Relationships (2651, 2650)]. This establishes a main grounding connection and facilitates the distribution of grounding connections from the TMGB to individual grounding points [Data: Entities (1953), Relationships (2651, 2650)]. The TBB is designed for both vertical and horizontal main connections and must be correctly sized to handle the total current it is expected to carry, ensuring the integrity and effectiveness of the grounding system [Data: Entities (1953), Relationships (2666)]. A diagram shows the TBB connected to a TGB, illustrating this physical connection within the grounding subsystem [Data: Relationships (2680)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"TGB: Grounding for Telecommunications Equipment\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The Telecommunications Grounding Busbar (TGB) is a critical component specifically designed for telecommunications facilities, providing a centralized and convenient grounding point within Telecommunications Rooms (SALAS DE TELECOMUNICAÇÕES) and equipment rooms [Data: Entities (1954), Relationships (89)]. It serves as a connection point for network equipment, ensuring proper grounding for all connected devices, such as hubs and patch panels [Data: Entities (1954), Relationships (2654, 2680)]. The TGB simplifies the grounding process for multiple pieces of equipment in a single location, facilitating efficient installation and maintenance of network infrastructure [Data: Entities (1954)]. Its operation requires a connection to either the TBB or the TMGB, highlighting its role within the larger grounding system [Data: Entities (1954)].\"\n    },\